<Project>
  <!--
    The Supabase export as a build step, for the project that sets SupabaseMigrationsExport: the host of a
    modular monolith, or the presentation layer of a service. In the project file:

      <SupabaseMigrationsExport>Write</SupabaseMigrationsExport>
      <SupabaseMigrationsExport Condition="'$(ContinuousIntegrationBuild)' == 'true'">Check</SupabaseMigrationsExport>

    Write writes every missing file after the build; Check only compares and fails the build when a file is
    missing, changed or orphaned. Leave it unset and nothing happens. SupabaseMigrationsDirectory overrides
    where the files go; by default the supabase/config.toml nearest above the project decides, the way the
    Supabase CLI finds its project.

    Two more properties say what the policies are written with, for a project whose roles or caller
    functions are not Supabase's own. Each is key=value pairs separated by '|', every pair optional, so no
    value can hold a '|'; each caller function is one called without arguments:

      <SupabaseRowAccessRoles>user=authenticated|anonymous=anon|system-in=ddd_system_in</SupabaseRowAccessRoles>
      <SupabaseCallerFunctions>uid=auth.uid()|role=auth.role()|claims=auth.jwt()</SupabaseCallerFunctions>

    The export runs before the application's Main, so its configuration cannot reach it; these properties
    are how it does. They must name the roles the application's row level security options configure, or
    the policies are for roles no query runs as.

    A token role the application maps to a database role of its own, TokenRoles in those options, is one
    more pair of the first property, token:<role>=<database role>, the token role spelled as the token
    spells it:

      <SupabaseRowAccessRoles>token:analyst=desk_analyst</SupabaseRowAccessRoles>

    A rule for RowAccessRoles.Token("analyst") is then written for desk_analyst; without the pair the export
    refuses the rule.

    The role the application's own bookkeeping runs as, SystemRole in those options, where the role it logs
    in as holds nothing, is one more pair as well, system=<database role>:

      <SupabaseRowAccessRoles>system=ddd_system</SupabaseRowAccessRoles>

    It names a role of the application's own, which the access files make. Leave it out where the system
    caller runs as service_role: the export refuses one of Postgres's or Supabase's own roles there.

    Two switches say what the access files write besides the policies, and both are on unless a project turns
    them off: every access file writes each table's privileges from its policies, and those of the outbox, the
    inbox and an event log, and writes FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY after every ENABLE, so a table's owner is held
    to its policies too. Turning one off is rare:

      <SupabaseRowAccessGrants>None</SupabaseRowAccessGrants>
      <SupabaseForceRowLevelSecurity>false</SupabaseForceRowLevelSecurity>

    None leaves the privileges to migrations written by hand, for a project that grants them itself and has to
    keep doing so. false leaves the tables' owner outside the policies, for a project whose own work runs as an
    owner that cannot bypass row level security; on Supabase the owner is postgres, which can.

    One more names the role the application logs in as, where that is a role of its own that owns nothing:

      <SupabaseLoginRole>sample_api</SupabaseLoginRole>

    The export then writes the migration that makes it, NOLOGIN NOINHERIT, and grants it the roles the first
    property maps and nothing else, after every other file; a new one whenever those roles change. The login
    and its password stay the deployment's, since a migration is kept in a repository. Unset, nothing is
    written.

    It shipped here, in buildTransitive, so the host gets it through the module that references this
    package; the host does not have to reference the package itself.

    The same goes for every other project that references this package, or a project that does: each
    imports this file. So the step runs only in a project that can be the host: an application, whose
    program the step can start, and not a test project, which is an application too. In any other
    project the property is left alone, so it may be given for a whole build, on the command line, in a
    Directory.Build.props or by CI: it reaches the host, and the modules and the tests ignore it. Every
    other application the build reaches runs the step as well, so a solution with more than one keeps
    the property in the host's project file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The generator writes the list of sources and the module initializer only where this is Write or Check,
      in an application that is not a test project: the projects this step runs in, and no others. The two
      properties that tell a test project are declared here, beside the step, so the two always decide alike.
    -->
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="SupabaseMigrationsExport" />
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="IsTestProject" />
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="IsTestingPlatformApplication" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="DDDToolkitSupabaseMigrations"
          AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="('$(SupabaseMigrationsExport)' == 'Write' Or '$(SupabaseMigrationsExport)' == 'Check') And ('$(OutputType)' == 'Exe' Or '$(OutputType)' == 'WinExe') And '$(IsTestProject)' != 'true' And '$(IsTestingPlatformApplication)' != 'true' And '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' And '$(BuildingForLiveUnitTesting)' != 'true'">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_DDDToolkitDotnet>$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)</_DDDToolkitDotnet>
      <_DDDToolkitDotnet Condition="'$(_DDDToolkitDotnet)' == ''">dotnet</_DDDToolkitDotnet>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!--
      The variables below are passed as one list that MSBuild splits at every ';', so a ';' in any of these
      properties would cut it short, and the rest would become variables of their own. Refused, naming the
      separator that works, or for the login role, which is one name, what a name is.
    -->
    <Error Condition="$(SupabaseRowAccessRoles.Contains(';'))"
           Text="SupabaseRowAccessRoles is '$(SupabaseRowAccessRoles)', with a ';'. Separate its pairs with '|' instead, as in user=authenticated|anonymous=anon|system-in=ddd_system_in: MSBuild splits the export's variables at every ';'." />
    <Error Condition="$(SupabaseCallerFunctions.Contains(';'))"
           Text="SupabaseCallerFunctions is '$(SupabaseCallerFunctions)', with a ';'. Separate its pairs with '|' instead, as in uid=auth.uid()|role=auth.role()|claims=auth.jwt(): MSBuild splits the export's variables at every ';'." />
    <Error Condition="$(SupabaseLoginRole.Contains(';'))"
           Text="SupabaseLoginRole is '$(SupabaseLoginRole)', with a ';'. It is the name of one role, a plain lowercase identifier such as sample_api: MSBuild splits the export's variables at every ';'." />

    <!--
      Runs the application that was just built. The generated module initializer sees the variable before
      Main, exports, and ends the process, so none of the application's own start-up runs: no host builder,
      no configuration providers, no hosted services.
    -->
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(_DDDToolkitDotnet)&quot; &quot;$(TargetPath)&quot;"
          WorkingDirectory="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)"
          EnvironmentVariables="DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_EXPORT=$(SupabaseMigrationsExport);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_DIRECTORY=$(SupabaseMigrationsDirectory);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_START=$(MSBuildProjectDirectory);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_ROLES=$(SupabaseRowAccessRoles);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_CALLER_FUNCTIONS=$(SupabaseCallerFunctions);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_GRANTS=$(SupabaseRowAccessGrants);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_FORCE=$(SupabaseForceRowLevelSecurity);DDDTOOLKIT_SUPABASE_LOGIN_ROLE=$(SupabaseLoginRole)" />
  </Target>
</Project>
